```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final CustomerRepository customerRepo; // required, immutable
    private PaymentGateway paymentGateway;         // optional

    // Constructor injection — preferred for required dependencies.
    // Since Spring 4.3, @Autowired is optional on a single constructor.
    @Autowired
    public OrderService(CustomerRepository customerRepo) {
        this.customerRepo = customerRepo;
    }

    // Setter injection — used for optional dependencies.
    @Autowired
    public void setPaymentGateway(PaymentGateway gateway) {
        this.paymentGateway = gateway;
    }

    public void processOrder(Long customerId, Order order) {
        Customer customer = customerRepo.findById(customerId);
        paymentGateway.charge(customer, order.getTotal());
    }
}

@Repository
public class CustomerRepository {
    public Customer findById(Long id) { /* JDBC logic */ return null; }
}
```

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    // Field injection: convenient to type, but the dependency is hidden
    // and the field can't be final. A plain unit test can't set it
    // without reflection or starting the whole Spring context.
    @Autowired
    private CustomerRepository customerRepo;

    public Customer load(Long id) {
        return customerRepo.findById(id); // NPE in a plain `new OrderService()` test
    }
}
```
Field injection compiles and runs under Spring, but it hides dependencies from callers and forces every unit test to spin up the container.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final CustomerRepository customerRepo; // final = immutable, always set

    public OrderService(CustomerRepository customerRepo) {
        this.customerRepo = customerRepo; // dependency is explicit in the signature
    }

    public Customer load(Long id) {
        return customerRepo.findById(id);
    }
}

// Plain unit test — no Spring context needed:
// var service = new OrderService(new FakeCustomerRepository());
```
Constructor injection makes the dependency explicit, allows `final` fields, prevents partially constructed objects, and works in plain JUnit tests.

```java
@Component
class StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway { /* ... */ }

@Component
class PayPalGateway implements PaymentGateway { /* ... */ }

@Service
class OrderService {
    // Two PaymentGateway beans exist and neither is @Primary.
    OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) { /* ... */ }
}
// Startup fails with:
// NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException: expected single matching bean but found 2
```
With two candidates and no hint, Spring can't pick one — it stops at startup.

```java
@Component("stripeGateway")
class StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway { /* ... */ }

@Component("payPalGateway")
class PayPalGateway implements PaymentGateway { /* ... */ }

@Service
class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;

    // Name the bean you want — Spring injects StripeGateway, not PayPalGateway.
    OrderService(@Qualifier("stripeGateway") PaymentGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }
}
```
The `@Qualifier` resolves the ambiguity by exact name, so the right implementation is injected.

```java
// No @Repository / @Component here, so Spring never registers a bean.
public class EmailService {
    public void send(String to, String body) { /* SMTP call */ }
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final EmailService emailService;

    public OrderService(EmailService emailService) { // Spring has no EmailService bean
        this.emailService = emailService;
    }
}
// Startup fails with:
// NoSuchBeanDefinitionException: No qualifying bean of type 'EmailService'
```
Without a stereotype annotation, `EmailService` is just a plain class — Spring can't inject what it never created.

```java
@Service // now Spring creates and manages an EmailService bean
public class EmailService {
    public void send(String to, String body) { /* SMTP call */ }
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final EmailService emailService;

    // Truly optional dependency: null when no bean exists.
    @Autowired(required = false)
    private MetricsCollector metricsCollector;

    public OrderService(EmailService emailService) {
        this.emailService = emailService; // resolves now
    }

    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        emailService.send(order.getCustomerEmail(), "Confirmed");
        if (metricsCollector != null) {       // guard the optional bean
            metricsCollector.recordOrderProcessed();
        }
    }
}
```
Marking the class `@Service` fixes the required dependency; `required = false` is only for genuinely optional beans like metrics.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final List<PaymentHandler> handlers;

    // Spring injects ALL PaymentHandler beans into the list.
    public PaymentService(List<PaymentHandler> handlers) {
        this.handlers = handlers;
    }

    public void processPayment(Order order) {
        for (PaymentHandler handler : handlers) {
            if (handler.supports(order.getPaymentType())) {
                handler.process(order);
                return;
            }
        }
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("No handler for " + order.getPaymentType());
    }
}

@Component
class CreditCardHandler implements PaymentHandler {
    public boolean supports(String type) { return "CREDIT_CARD".equals(type); }
    public void process(Order order) { /* Stripe API call */ }
}

@Component
class PayPalHandler implements PaymentHandler {
    public boolean supports(String type) { return "PAYPAL".equals(type); }
    public void process(Order order) { /* PayPal API call */ }
}
```

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    // Every handler wired by name. Adding a new one means editing this class.
    @Autowired private CreditCardHandler creditCard;
    @Autowired private PayPalHandler payPal;

    public void process(Order order) {
        if ("CREDIT_CARD".equals(order.getPaymentType())) creditCard.process(order);
        else if ("PAYPAL".equals(order.getPaymentType())) payPal.process(order);
        // ...another branch for every new handler
    }
}
```
This hardcodes each implementation, so adding a payment method forces you to edit the service — it isn't open to extension.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final List<PaymentHandler> handlers; // Spring injects them all

    public PaymentService(List<PaymentHandler> handlers) {
        this.handlers = handlers;
    }

    public void process(Order order) {
        handlers.stream()
            .filter(h -> h.supports(order.getPaymentType()))
            .findFirst()
            .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalArgumentException("No handler"))
            .process(order);
    }
}
// Add a new @Component handler later and PaymentService never changes.
```
Now the service depends only on the interface; dropping in a new `@Component` handler extends behaviour without touching this class.

```java
@Configuration
public class DataSourceConfig {

    @Bean
    @Primary  // default DataSource for most repositories
    public DataSource mysqlDataSource() {
        HikariDataSource ds = new HikariDataSource();
        ds.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/main");
        return ds;
    }

    @Bean("analyticsDataSource")
    public DataSource postgresDataSource() {
        HikariDataSource ds = new HikariDataSource();
        ds.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/analytics");
        return ds;
    }
}

@Repository
public class OrderRepository {
    private final DataSource dataSource;
    // Gets mysqlDataSource because it is @Primary — no @Qualifier needed.
    public OrderRepository(DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = dataSource;
    }
}

@Repository
public class AnalyticsRepository {
    private final DataSource dataSource;
    // Explicitly requests the alternative — @Qualifier overrides @Primary.
    public AnalyticsRepository(@Qualifier("analyticsDataSource") DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = dataSource;
    }
}
```

```java
// Two CacheService beans exist. Without @Primary, every injection point
// must name the bean, or Spring throws NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException.
@Service
public class ProductService {
    private final CacheService cache;
    public ProductService(@Qualifier("redisCacheService") CacheService cache) {
        this.cache = cache; // same qualifier copied into dozens of classes
    }
}
```
Repeating `@Qualifier("redisCacheService")` in every class is noisy and breaks the moment someone renames the bean.

```java
@Service
@Primary // the default CacheService for the whole app, declared once
public class RedisCacheService implements CacheService { /* ... */ }

@Service
public class InMemoryCacheService implements CacheService { /* ... */ }

@Service
public class ProductService {
    private final CacheService cache;
    // No @Qualifier needed — Spring injects the @Primary RedisCacheService.
    public ProductService(CacheService cache) {
        this.cache = cache;
    }
}
```
One `@Primary` removes the qualifier from every common injection point; only the rare class that needs `InMemoryCacheService` adds a `@Qualifier`.
